<mujoco model="so101">
  <!--
    Local modifications of the MuJoCo Menagerie robotstudio_so101 model (Apache 2.0):
    1. Wrist camera physical intrinsics (sensorsize/focal) removed and an explicit
       fovy added, so the simulator's fovy-based FOV randomization is honored
       (MuJoCo ignores fovy when physical intrinsics are present).
   2. gripperframe site quat changed from "1 0 1 0" to "0 0 1 0" so its local z
      points along the gripper "forward" direction (the library convention used
      by TCP-orientation observations). The look-at task uses the wrist camera
      optical axis instead. See PROVENANCE.md.
    See PROVENANCE.md.
  -->
  <option integrator="implicitfast" timestep="0.005" cone="elliptic" iterations="10" ls_iterations="20" impratio="10"/>

  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <visual>
    <scale contactwidth="0.1" contactheight="0.05" forcewidth="0.02" framelength="0.75" framewidth="0.025"
      camera="0.2"/>
  </visual>

  <default>
    <mesh maxhullvert="128"/>

    <default class="so101">
      <joint damping="1" frictionloss="0.1" armature="0.005"/>
      <position kp="50"/>
      <default class="visual">
        <geom type="mesh" contype="0" conaffinity="0" group="2"/>
      </default>
      <default class="collision">
        <geom group="3" condim="3"/>
      </default>
      <default class="collision_gripper">
        <!-- Sliding friction (2026-08-13): was 1 (MuJoCo engine default,
             never a real estimate). Changed to 0.45, informed by a real
             tribology measurement of PLA-on-PLA static friction (0.47-0.62,
             https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Static-friction-coefficient-
             for-PLA_fig7_398087395), the correct real-world pairing since
             these jaw fingertips are bare 3D-printed plastic with no rubber/
             silicone grip pad anywhere in this file's mesh/material names.
             0.45 sits just under the measured static range as a kinetic-
             friction estimate (kinetic is typically <= static). Not a
             measurement of this exact printed part -- an estimate
             substituted for an arbitrary default, not hardware calibration. -->
        <geom group="3" condim="6" friction="0.45 5e-3 5e-4" solref="0.01 1" priority="1" rgba="1.0 0 0 1.0" mass="0"/>
      </default>
      <default class="collision_gripper_mesh">
        <!-- Collision DISABLED (2026-08-11 session): these are maxhullvert="64" single
             convex hulls of the jaw claw parts (so101_assets/so101.xml mesh declarations).
             A convex hull of a concave pincer shape fills in the gripping notch, so any
             cube placed "inside" the intended pinch gap instead hits a solid wedge --
             confirmed empirically: with this mesh active, EVERY tested closing angle from
             1.0-3.5deg produced high-force (up to 5.9N), high-penetration (3-5mm) contact
             that ejected the cube rather than holding it (see grasp-geometry investigation,
             sweep_close_qpos*.py). Disabling it and relying on the primitive sphere/box/
             capsule tip geoms (class="collision_gripper", still fully active) for contact
             produced a genuinely stable pinch across qpos 1.0-1.9deg. Geometry is still
             used for rendering (group=4, visual+collision groups unaffected by contype). -->
        <geom group="4" condim="6" friction="1 5e-3 5e-4" solref="0.01 1" priority="1" rgba="1.0 0 0 1.0" mass="0"
          contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      </default>
    </default>

    <default class="sts3215">
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      <joint damping="0.60" frictionloss="0.052" armature="0.028"/>
      <!-- Position gains FITTED TO THIS ARM (2026-08-26, Task 2).

           Previous values were kp=998.22 kv=2.731, calculated from
           https://github.com/Gregory119/RBE501-RL-arm-project/blob/main/gymnasium_env/README.md
           assuming a servo proportional gain of 16. That derivation is not a
           1-to-1 mapping of the LeRobot servo gains and was never checked
           against hardware; measured against this arm it is badly
           underdamped. On a 20-unit elbow step the old gains drove the sim to
           23.8 deg against a 19.6 deg target - a 4.2 deg overshoot with
           visible ringing - and peaked at 243 deg/s where the real joint
           manages 150 deg/s.

           These values come from a grid fit against real recorded step
           responses (scripts/characterization/tune_actuator.py), fitted on
           the three step experiments and validated on the ramp, reverse and
           triangle runs held out of the fit:

               elbow_flex RMSE   fit      1.148 -> 0.366 deg  (68% better)
                                 held-out 1.470 -> 0.561 deg  (62% better)

           Held-out error improving alongside fit error is what distinguishes
           a real fit from curve-fitting noise. Peak overshoot on the 20-unit
           step drops from 8.05 deg to 2.19 deg.

           The RMSE surface is a flat valley along kp/kv ~ 16, so the ratio is
           what matters, not the absolute magnitudes - any pair on that ridge
           scores within 0.01 deg. kp=400 sits at the minimum.

           CAVEAT: fitted against elbow_flex only, the one joint characterised
           so far, and applied to the whole sts3215 class because every joint
           on this arm uses the same servo. The shoulder carries far more
           inertia and may want its own fit; re-run tune_actuator.py per joint
           as each is characterised. -->
      <position kp="400" kv="25" forcerange="-2.94 2.94"/>
    </default>

    <default class="backlash">
      <!-- +/- 0.5° of backlash – not added to any joints for now -->
      <joint damping="0.01" frictionloss="0" armature="0.01" limited="true" range="-0.0087267 0.0087267"/>
    </default>
  </default>

